We will do casework on face <math>ABCD.</math> 

'''Case 1:''' Two adjacent edges on face <math>ABCD</math> are both labeled <math>1</math>. 

There are <math>4</math> ways to choose two adjacent edges on the top face. WLOG say we choose edges <math>BC</math> and <math>CD</math> to be labeled <math>1.</math> Then <math>AB</math> and <math>AD</math> must be labeled <math>0.</math> 

We have three subcases for face <math>BCGF:</math> 

'''SC1:''' <math>FG</math> is labeled <math>1</math> 

Then the rest of the cube becomes uniquely determined.

'''SC2:''' <math>CG</math> is labeled <math>1</math> 

The cube still becomes uniquely determined.

'''SC3:''' <math>BF</math> is labeled <math>1</math> 

If <math>BF</math> is labeled <math>1,</math> we can choose either <math>DH</math> or <math>GH</math> to be labeled <math>1</math> on face <math>CDHG.</math> Then the rest of the cube is uniquely determined. 

In total we get <math>4(1 + 1 + 2) = 16</math> ways for this case. 

'''Case 2:''' Two opposite edges on face <math>ABCD</math> are both labeled <math>1</math> 

There are <math>2</math> ways to choose the opposite edges. WLOG say we choose edges <math>BC</math> and <math>AD.</math> Then for face <math>BCGF</math> we can choose either <math>CG</math> or <math>BF</math> to be labeled <math>1</math> (we can't choose <math>FG</math> because that would force an invalid labeling). 

This case gives us <math>2 \cdot 2 = 4</math> ways. 

So the answer is <math>16 + 4 = \boxed{20}.</math> 

